00:00:18.966 --> 00:00:20.340
One of the challenges
with making

00:00:20.340 --> 00:00:23.010
modern cloud-native applications
is the amount of strain

00:00:23.010 --> 00:00:25.050
that it can put on
your DevOps teams.

00:00:25.050 --> 00:00:28.530
This is why we need strong tools
to do monitoring, discovery,

00:00:28.530 --> 00:00:30.480
fault tolerance,
and observability

00:00:30.480 --> 00:00:33.540
for microservice deployments,
no matter where they run.

00:00:33.540 --> 00:00:35.040
In this session,
we brought together

00:00:35.040 --> 00:00:37.590
folks from Google Cloud and
Lyft to share what we've

00:00:37.590 --> 00:00:40.090
done together in this space.

00:00:40.090 --> 00:00:43.210
First let's talk about Istio,
an open source service mesh

00:00:43.210 --> 00:00:46.150
that makes it easy to create
a network of deployed services

00:00:46.150 --> 00:00:48.340
with load balancing,
service-to-service

00:00:48.340 --> 00:00:50.680
authentication,
monitoring, and more

00:00:50.680 --> 00:00:53.287
without any changes
in service code.

00:00:53.287 --> 00:00:55.120
This is especially
useful when transitioning

00:00:55.120 --> 00:00:58.570
from monolithic applications
to a distributed microservice

00:00:58.570 --> 00:00:59.530
architecture.

00:00:59.530 --> 00:01:01.240
A few miles down the
road from Google,

00:01:01.240 --> 00:01:04.060
ride-sharing company Lyft
was facing similar challenges

00:01:04.060 --> 00:01:05.890
with their move to cloud native.

00:01:05.890 --> 00:01:08.350
As they built microservices
in multiple languages,

00:01:08.350 --> 00:01:10.510
it became much harder
to create a common proxy

00:01:10.510 --> 00:01:11.900
to support each language.

00:01:11.900 --> 00:01:13.270
So they developed Envoy--

00:01:13.270 --> 00:01:16.420
an open source distributed
proxy for large service mesh

00:01:16.420 --> 00:01:19.840
architectures giving DevOps
eventually consistent service

00:01:19.840 --> 00:01:23.110
discovery and observability
even for microservices

00:01:23.110 --> 00:01:24.670
written in different languages.

00:01:24.670 --> 00:01:28.690
Envoy used very similar APIs
to internal Google APIs,

00:01:28.690 --> 00:01:31.880
so it became a natural fit
to build it into Istio.

00:01:31.880 --> 00:01:35.660
Istio is comprised of a data
plane and a control plane.

00:01:35.660 --> 00:01:37.720
Envoy provides the
data plane with a set

00:01:37.720 --> 00:01:39.370
of intelligent
proxies to control

00:01:39.370 --> 00:01:42.080
all network communication
between microservices.

00:01:42.080 --> 00:01:45.310
while the control plane manages
the proxies to route traffic

00:01:45.310 --> 00:01:47.110
and enforce policies.

00:01:47.110 --> 00:01:49.000
This now makes it
possible to make a service

00:01:49.000 --> 00:01:52.120
available to a single
Google Kubernetes cluster

00:01:52.120 --> 00:01:54.170
and across multiple clusters.

00:01:54.170 --> 00:01:57.670
So with Istio and Envoy,
we get load balancing

00:01:57.670 --> 00:02:00.710
across microservices
in a same region.

00:02:00.710 --> 00:02:03.310
But we still need the ability
to balance between regions

00:02:03.310 --> 00:02:04.510
as well.

00:02:04.510 --> 00:02:07.660
Thankfully, Traffic
Director is here to help.

00:02:07.660 --> 00:02:11.110
It's a GCP-managed service that
uses open APIs to integrate

00:02:11.110 --> 00:02:13.540
with Istio and
Envoy's service proxy

00:02:13.540 --> 00:02:16.780
to give you global load
balancing for your backends.

00:02:16.780 --> 00:02:19.900
This is really awesome because
you can now expand services

00:02:19.900 --> 00:02:22.240
and spill over across regions.

00:02:22.240 --> 00:02:24.460
It gives you centralized
health checking, meaning

00:02:24.460 --> 00:02:27.370
each proxy doesn't need
to check one another.

00:02:27.370 --> 00:02:29.800
Lastly, it enables
traffic-driven autoscaling

00:02:29.800 --> 00:02:31.870
for your containerized backends.

00:02:31.870 --> 00:02:34.060
Traffic moves from one
microservice and run

00:02:34.060 --> 00:02:36.700
region to the next service
in a different region,

00:02:36.700 --> 00:02:38.740
acting like a
global load balancer

00:02:38.740 --> 00:02:41.390
but for your own
internal services.

00:02:41.390 --> 00:02:44.200
Now your developers can continue
to focus on business logic

00:02:44.200 --> 00:02:46.455
without a PhD in provisioning.

00:02:46.455 --> 00:02:48.580
